A||||5||fiero56||water pump replacement||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-12-2000||10:53 PM||||I worked on the water pump replacement on my Formula again today. I don't know what GM was smoking when they decided to use Torx bolts on the later model v-6s. I finally gave up trying to re-install the Torx bolts and bought hex bolt equivalents at Sears. Much easier to keep a socket on than a Torx bit. Both of my '86 v-6s use regular bolts. I haven't figured how to plug the pipe leading from the water pump. I already returned the original for my core charge and forgot to remove the plug from the pump housing <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. Almost finished, have just another bolt or two to install, install belt, fix the vacuum lines I broke, a few other small things, and it will be be in commission. I will go insane if I didn't get the seal right and it leaks after it pressurises. Now all I have left after the pump is upper ball joint and throttle cable replancement. I can't wait to drive a Fiero with good sounding speakers again.<P>W/P replacement on a 6 is bad, I can't imagine replacing one on a 4.||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||12-12-2000||11:04 PM||||Drew, take the pipe out and plug the hole with a 1/2" plug found at an Home Depot or equivalent plumbing department. It's just a 1/2" pipe plug.........smooth move on the torx to hex head replacement!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-12-2000||11:25 PM||||Hi Terry. Thanks for the plug sizing. I didn't think I would ever find what size plug would be needed, so I had these wierd plans on rubber hose plugs, etc. Glad to hear I can get a plug almost anywhere. I haven't started the Formula in over two weeks, hope it fires up, I don't have a lot of confidence in it's current 2.8.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||12-13-2000||02:30 AM||||Actually the 4 cylinder is easy, I hear ya on the torx bolts, I used to be the W/P guy at the shop. I just got good by repetition. It was the GT fastbacks I was always afraid of, with the buttress coming down it was often difficult to get the top bolts without being afraid of putting a ratchet through the quarterwindow or leaning on it too much to crack it...<P>After the water pump is put on and the system has been filled I always liked to start the Fiero and let it go through a heat cycle (warm up to the point the fan comes on by itself and when the fan turns itself off.) and look for leaks. Make sure you tefloned the bolts that go through the water jacket!||||reg||14
Z||000004||fiero56||12-21-2000||12:50 PM||||I worked a little on the Formula again today. Tensioned the belt, filled the system. Now to install EGR solonoid, battery back in, and I think I'm done. Run it, check for leaks, retension the belt, burp the system, decklid back on and it's finished. I haven't been working on the Formula much, that's why it has taken this long. Been having too much fun with a smooth Muncie and a strong pulling 2.8.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-21-2000||02:04 PM||||Well, I fired it up this afternoon. Been just over a month since I last started the engine. Fired right up, first try.<P>Wicked Fiero, I didn't remeber which bolts went through the water jacket, now I know which ones <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. It dripped all over the place. I'll tape them up soon. The seal looks like it is holding and not leaking. That's good 'cause I did a terrible job cleaning up the surfaces for the new one.<P>I missed the stereo in the Formula too. I sat in the car for a few minutes and listned to Guerrilla Radio by RATM(on the radio). Can't wait for an aftermarket headunit, and new speakers in back. But the 6x9s are <B>excellent</B>, I didn't remember them sounding so good.||||reg||1
